From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bootowser <b@o.local>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 17:00:14 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] [Bootowser] docshell: enforce a top-level navigation
 allowlist

--kiosk removes the browser chrome, but nothing in it stops the *page*
from navigating away: a link, a redirect, a form target or a script can
replace what is on screen. Once the kiosk is showing attacker-chosen
content, the policy file is all that is left holding the line -- and
policy is a runtime control an operator can weaken.

Enterprise policy can block about:* and chrome://* through URLBlocklist,
but it will not automatically cover schemes Firefox grows later, and it
is trivially removable from a file on disk.

So enforce the allowlist in the source, at nsDocShell::LoadURI() and
nsDocShell::InternalLoad() -- the two choke points every top-level
navigation passes through, before a channel is created. Both are
checked because history and bfcache navigation reach InternalLoad()
without going through LoadURI().

The "top-level" part matters: sub-frames reach the same code and are
left alone, so pages may embed what they like but can never steer the
kiosk itself out of the web.

The allowlist is http/https, plus the handful of things the browser
loads *into its own top-level window* and cannot work without:
chrome:// (the window and browser UI documents -- without this the
browser never paints itself), about:blank/newtab/home, and
moz-extension:// for bundled extensions' background pages. None of
those are reachable from page content on their own: nsSecurityManager
already refuses a non-chrome caller a top-level chrome:// navigation,
and moz-extension: needs host permission the content lacks.

Getting this list wrong is not theoretical. The first cut allowed only
http/https/about:blank, and launching the build logged
"blocked top-level navigation to chrome://global/content/win.xhtml" --
a browser that cannot draw its own window. about:newtab and the bundled
extensions' background pages broke the same way.

Blocked navigations fail with NS_ERROR_ABORT, which the callers already
know how to report: the page sees an aborted navigation rather than a
half-loaded privileged document.

Also registers the "bootowser" static pref group in modules/libpref's
moz.build. StaticPrefsAll.h includes a header per group, so a group
declared in StaticPrefList.yaml but absent from that list fails the
build with "mozilla/StaticPrefs_bootowser.h file not found" rather than
being quietly skipped.

Guarded by MOZ_BOOTOWSER, set by the new --enable-bootowser flag, so
upstream builds are unaffected.
---
 docshell/base/nsDocShell.cpp | 122 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 docshell/base/nsDocShell.h   |   5 ++
 modules/libpref/moz.build    |  10 +++
 toolkit/moz.configure        |  17 +++++
 4 files changed, 154 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docshell/base/nsDocShell.cpp b/docshell/base/nsDocShell.cpp
index 6fe906d343..8f45f0ca95 100644
--- a/docshell/base/nsDocShell.cpp
+++ b/docshell/base/nsDocShell.cpp
@@ -714,6 +714,121 @@ nsresult nsDocShell::CheckDisallowedJavascriptLoad(
   return NS_ERROR_DOM_BAD_CROSS_ORIGIN_URI;
 }
 
+// [Bootowser] Cancel top-level navigations that are not plain web content.
+//
+// Firefox's --kiosk removes the chrome, but nothing in it stops the *page*
+// from navigating: a link, a redirect, a form target or a script can replace
+// what is on screen, and once the kiosk is showing attacker-chosen content the
+// policy file is the only thing still holding the line. Enterprise policy can
+// block about:* and chrome://* through URLBlocklist, but that is a runtime
+// control an operator can weaken, and it will not automatically cover schemes
+// Firefox grows later.
+//
+// So enforce the allowlist here, at the single choke point every top-level
+// navigation passes through, before any channel is created. Kiosk mode is meant
+// to be a one-way door, and this is the door.
+//
+// Sub-frames are deliberately untouched: a page may embed whatever it likes,
+// but it can never navigate the kiosk itself out of the web.
+nsresult nsDocShell::CheckBootowserNavigation(nsDocShellLoadState* aLoadState) {
+  // Compiled in only by the Bootowser build (see firefox/patches in the
+  // Bootowser repository), so upstream builds are unaffected.
+#ifdef MOZ_BOOTOWSER
+  // Only the top-level document matters. A sub-frame load reaches here with a
+  // non-top BrowsingContext, and blocking it would break ordinary embedded
+  // content (ads, iframes, third-party scripts).
+  if (!mBrowsingContext || !mBrowsingContext->IsTop()) {
+    return NS_OK;
+  }
+
+  nsIURI* uri = aLoadState->URI();
+  if (!uri) {
+    return NS_ERROR_MALFORMED_URI;
+  }
+
+  // http/https is the whole point of a web kiosk.
+  if (uri->SchemeIs("http") || uri->SchemeIs("https")) {
+    return NS_OK;
+  }
+
+  // chrome:// is Firefox's own UI, and two of those documents *are* the window:
+  // the browser loads them at startup, and refusing them leaves a window that
+  // never paints its own UI. Those two are the whole exemption -- by exact
+  // host and path, not by scheme.
+  //
+  // A page cannot reach a chrome: URL at all: nsSecurityManager refuses a
+  // non-chrome caller a top-level chrome: navigation ("Not allowed to load
+  // privileged URL") long before a docshell is involved. The person at the
+  // machine can type one, though, and that puts a privileged document on
+  // screen. Refining the allowlist to the two window documents is what closes
+  // the rest of the scheme -- chrome://mozapps/, chrome://global/content/dialog*,
+  // and every other chrome page stop being navigable.
+  //
+  // The two exempt documents stay reachable in a tab, which is the residue of
+  // this trade: a typed chrome://browser/content/browser.xhtml still gives a
+  // second copy of the browser UI. It is not an escalation, because it is the
+  // same compiled-in lockdown and the same allow-list, but it is not nothing,
+  // and docs/security.md says so.
+  if (uri->SchemeIs("chrome")) {
+    // nsIURI has no path accessor, so compare the spec with any query and
+    // fragment cut off: the two window documents are addressed by path, and
+    // anything else on this scheme falls through to the refusal below.
+    nsAutoCString spec;
+    uri->GetSpec(spec);
+    int32_t cut = spec.FindChar('#');
+    if (cut >= 0) {
+      spec.Truncate(cut);
+    }
+    cut = spec.FindChar('?');
+    if (cut >= 0) {
+      spec.Truncate(cut);
+    }
+    if (spec == "chrome://global/content/win.xhtml" ||
+        spec == "chrome://browser/content/browser.xhtml") {
+      return NS_OK;
+    }
+  }
+
+  // about:newtab is the browser's own new-tab page, not a privileged document:
+  // it is HTML with no chrome privileges, and it is the page a startup race or a
+  // "open new tab" action lands on. Blocking it is only a self-inflicted blank
+  // window, so allow it.
+  if (uri->SchemeIs("about")) {
+    if (NS_IsAboutBlank(uri)) {
+      return NS_OK;
+    }
+    nsAutoCString spec;
+    uri->GetSpec(spec);
+    if (spec == "about:newtab" || spec == "about:home") {
+      return NS_OK;
+    }
+  }
+
+  // moz-extension:// is how bundled extensions (the PDF viewer, pocket, the
+  // default-theme and system-addon shims) load their background pages. Those are
+  // part of making the browser work; refusing them breaks PDF viewing and
+  // similar. A web page still cannot reach an extension's privileged page on its
+  // own -- moz-extension: URIs are not loadable from content without the
+  // extension being granted host permission first, which policy controls.
+  if (uri->SchemeIs("moz-extension")) {
+    return NS_OK;
+  }
+
+  // Everything else is refused: about:config, about:addons, devtools://,
+  // view-source:, resource://, file://, and any scheme added upstream later.
+  //
+  // NS_ERROR_ABORT is what the caller already knows how to report: the load
+  // simply fails, and the page sees an aborted navigation rather than a
+  // half-loaded privileged document.
+  MOZ_LOG(gNavigationAPILog, LogLevel::Warning,
+          ("[bootowser] blocked top-level navigation to %s",
+           uri->GetSpecOrDefault().get()));
+  return NS_ERROR_ABORT;
+#else
+  return NS_OK;
+#endif
+}
+
 NS_IMETHODIMP
 nsDocShell::LoadURI(nsDocShellLoadState* aLoadState, bool aSetNavigating) {
   return LoadURI(aLoadState, aSetNavigating, false);
@@ -740,6 +855,9 @@ nsresult nsDocShell::LoadURI(nsDocShellLoadState* aLoadState,
 
   MOZ_TRY(CheckDisallowedJavascriptLoad(aLoadState));
 
+  // [Bootowser] Enforce the kiosk navigation allowlist.
+  MOZ_TRY(CheckBootowserNavigation(aLoadState));
+
   bool oldIsNavigating = mIsNavigating;
   auto cleanupIsNavigating =
       MakeScopeExit([&]() { mIsNavigating = oldIsNavigating; });
@@ -8544,6 +8662,10 @@ nsresult nsDocShell::InternalLoad(nsDocShellLoadState* aLoadState,
 
   MOZ_TRY(CheckDisallowedJavascriptLoad(aLoadState));
 
+  // [Bootowser] InternalLoad is reachable without passing through LoadURI
+  // (history and bfcache navigation), so the allowlist is enforced here too.
+  MOZ_TRY(CheckBootowserNavigation(aLoadState));
+
   // If we don't have a target, we're loading into ourselves, and our load
   // delegate may want to intercept that load.
   SameDocumentNavigationState sameDocumentNavigationState;
diff --git a/docshell/base/nsDocShell.h b/docshell/base/nsDocShell.h
index c3fcd2985a..1183846497 100644
--- a/docshell/base/nsDocShell.h
+++ b/docshell/base/nsDocShell.h
@@ -1063,6 +1063,11 @@ class nsDocShell final : public nsDocLoader,
   // NS_ERROR_DOM_BAD_CROSS_ORIGIN_URI if it does not.
   nsresult CheckDisallowedJavascriptLoad(nsDocShellLoadState* aLoadState);
 
+  // [Bootowser] If MOZ_BOOTOWSER is defined, cancels top-level navigations to
+  // anything other than http/https and about:blank, before any load starts.
+  // Sub-frame loads are always permitted.
+  nsresult CheckBootowserNavigation(nsDocShellLoadState* aLoadState);
+
   nsresult LoadURI(nsDocShellLoadState* aLoadState, bool aSetNavigating,
                    bool aContinueHandlingSubframeHistory);
 
diff --git a/modules/libpref/moz.build b/modules/libpref/moz.build
index 2c8b218c61..724b50d433 100644
--- a/modules/libpref/moz.build
+++ b/modules/libpref/moz.build
@@ -30,6 +30,16 @@ pref_groups = [
     "apz",
     "beacon",
     "bidi",
+    # [Bootowser] The bootowser.build static pref is declared in
+    # StaticPrefList.yaml, so its generated header has to be built and
+    # installed here too. Without this, StaticPrefsAll.h includes
+    # mozilla/StaticPrefs_bootowser.h, which is never generated, and the
+    # build fails in modules/libpref with "file not found".
+    #
+    # Note this list is kept in sync with the "Prefs starting with ..." section
+    # headers in StaticPrefList.yaml; a group present in one and not the other
+    # fails the build rather than being ignored.
+    "bootowser",
     "browser",
     "channelclassifier",
     "clipboard",
diff --git a/toolkit/moz.configure b/toolkit/moz.configure
index 2a0b994468..a40484d32b 100644
--- a/toolkit/moz.configure
+++ b/toolkit/moz.configure
@@ -2061,6 +2061,23 @@ option(
 set_config("MOZ_REQUIRE_SIGNING", True, when="MOZ_REQUIRE_SIGNING")
 set_define("MOZ_REQUIRE_SIGNING", True, when="MOZ_REQUIRE_SIGNING")
 
+# ==============================================================
+# Bootowser kiosk lockdown
+# ==============================================================
+# When enabled, code that only makes sense for a single-purpose unattended
+# kiosk is compiled in: the top-level navigation allowlist, and the removal of
+# the UI escape hatches that kiosk mode leaves behind. Off by default, so an
+# upstream build is byte-for-byte unaffected.
+option(
+    "--enable-bootowser",
+    env="MOZ_BOOTOWSER",
+    default=False,
+    help="Build a locked-down Bootowser kiosk browser",
+)
+
+set_config("MOZ_BOOTOWSER", True, when="MOZ_BOOTOWSER")
+set_define("MOZ_BOOTOWSER", True, when="MOZ_BOOTOWSER")
+
 option(
     "--with-unsigned-addon-scopes",
     nargs="+",
